You can view and filter selectors in Kubernetes using the --show-labels and -l (or --selector) flags in kubectl. Selectors rely on the key-value labels attached to Kubernetes resources like Pods, Services, and Deployments.


1. View Existing Labels on Resources

To find out which labels are available to use as selectors, list the labels attached to your resources.
• Show all labels in a table column: Run kubectl get pods --show-labels to add a LABELS column to the standard output.

• Extract a specific label as a column: Run kubectl get pods -L app,env to display the values of the app and env keys in their own columns.

• Inspect full resource metadata: Run kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and look under the Labels section.

2. Filter Resources Using Selectors (-l)

Once you know the labels, use the -l flag to filter resources with equality-based or set-based selectors.
• Match an exact key-value pair: Run kubectl get pods -l app=nginx to return only Pods labeled with app: nginx.

• Exclude a specific label value: Run kubectl get pods -l env!=production to return all Pods where env is not production.

• Match multiple conditions (AND logic): Separate labels with a comma, such as kubectl get pods -l app=nginx,tier=frontend.

• Match multiple values (OR / Set-based logic): Use in or notin inside quotes, such as kubectl get pods -l 'env in (dev, staging)'.

-l = selectors

kubectl get pods --show-labels
kubectl get pods -L app,env
kubectl get pods -l app=nginx
kubectl get pods -l env!=production
kubectl get pods -l app=nginx,tier=frontend
kubectl get pods -l 'env in (dev, staging)'

kubectl get pods --selector env=dev
#you can also use --selector instead -l

#this will list all the objects in cluster(pods, services, replicasets, etc..)
kubectl get all

#this will list all the objects in cluster that having prod environment
kubectl get all -l env=prod

#you can also directly get the count of pods in prod env using word count + -l (line, no of line)
kubectl get all -l env=prod | wc -l
8

this will also take into consideration of headers(NAME, READY, STATUS, RESTARTS, AGE), to remove headers and count
kubectl get pods --selector env=prod --no-headers
kubectl get pods --selector env=prod --no-headers | wc -l



#Identify the POD which is part of the prod environment, the finance BU and of frontend tier(AND logic)
kubectl get pods -l env=prod,bu=finance,tier=frontend